diff --git a/doc/source/admin/resource-options.rst b/doc/source/admin/resource-options.rst
index 0fa3ec2..4e1d0ac 100644
--- a/doc/source/admin/resource-options.rst
+++ b/doc/source/admin/resource-options.rst
@@ -219,6 +219,74 @@ authenticate.
 
 See :ref:`multi_factor_authentication` for further details.
 
+.. _password_policy_exempt:
+
+password_policy_exempt
+----------------------
+
+Type: ``Boolean``
+
+For SQL users, explicitly classify a service identity as exempt from the
+``password_regex``, ``unique_last_password_count``, and
+``minimum_password_age`` checks. Only JSON ``true`` enables this option;
+``false`` or absence applies the ordinary password policy. JSON ``null``
+removes the option. Strings and numbers are not accepted as Boolean values.
+The SQL resource option identifier is ``PPEX``; no database migration is needed.
+
+Creating or updating a user while supplying this option (including ``false``
+and ``null``) additionally requires
+``identity:manage_password_policy_exemption``. Its default rule is
+``role:admin and system_scope:all`` without a deprecated admin fallback.
+Ordinary user-create/update authorization is still required. Deployments may
+explicitly override this rule for a dedicated deployment identity, for example::
+
+    identity:manage_password_policy_exemption: "role:admin and user_id:DEPLOYMENT_USER_ID and project_id:DEPLOYMENT_PROJECT_ID"
+
+The policy supports system, domain and project scopes to allow such explicit
+operator overrides; the default check only allows system administrators.
+Treat permission to set this option as a security administration privilege.
+
+The classification belongs to the target user, not its name, roles, domain or
+caller. When updating a password and options together, password validation
+uses the resulting options. Unspecified options are retained. Removing the
+exemption and supplying a password in the same request immediately applies the
+ordinary regex. Existing passwords are not retrospectively revalidated.
+Accepted classification assignments (including unchanged values and null
+removal) are recorded with their resulting classification in the identity log,
+with target and initiator identifiers, and in the existing CADF create/update
+notification reason when ``notification_format = cadf``. Passwords are never
+included. SQL password writes read the classification inside their transaction;
+updates and self-service changes lock the user row before reading the options.
+A stale manager cache cannot grant a password-policy exemption. Other identity
+drivers retain manager-side regex validation unless they explicitly implement
+the ``password_policy_validation_in_driver`` capability.
+
+This option does not disable first-use changes, password expiry or lockout.
+For a service identity requiring all these exceptions, send the password and
+all four options together at creation or update:
+
+.. code-block:: json
+
+   {
+       "user": {
+           "name": "service-identity",
+           "domain_id": "SERVICE_DOMAIN_ID",
+           "password": "SERVICE_PASSWORD_FROM_SECRET_STORE",
+           "options": {
+               "password_policy_exempt": true,
+               "ignore_change_password_upon_first_use": true,
+               "ignore_password_expiry": true,
+               "ignore_lockout_failure_attempts": true
+           }
+       }
+   }
+
+Self-service password changes still authenticate the original password and
+respect ``lock_password``. Password types, technical length limits, account
+state checks, revocation and cache invalidation continue to apply. The SQL
+backend skips history/minimum-age validation for exempt identities; external
+identity stores retain their own password controls.
+
